; `+` in the numeric plane, and the crossing back out.
;
; Two claims at once. The framing reaches every quantity WRITTEN inside it, so
; the sum is 11 + 12 + 13 rather than 10 + 6 — and the answer reaches the model
; as text, which is the only way a computed number gets into what is asked.
;
; A name is not written, so it is not shifted: the 5 takes the framing once
; where it stands, and both uses of `n` read what it settled to. If either rule
; slipped, the arithmetic in the prompt would be wrong and the model would be
; asked something else — so the claim is pinned to the PROMPT, which is the
; deterministic half. A small model is unreliable at echoing, and that is not a
; property of the language.
; expect: calls 1
; expect: clean
; expect: contains 36 30
; expect: answers
($ "Reply with exactly the two numbers below, separated by one space, and nothing else.\n"
   |(+ 10 ([sum] 1 2 3))|
   " "
   |(+ 10 (= n 5 ($ n n)))|)
